"""Android apps on the Pinebook Pro's ChromiumOS, through Waydroid.

`pinebook-android daemon` is the root service behind http://android.localhost
and the `android` command. The upstart job pinebook-android starts it when a
user logs in. It does everything Waydroid needs on ChromiumOS:

setup   On first use, adds an ANDROID-DATA partition (ext4) in the free space
        after the ChromiumOS partitions of the card the system booted from and
        mounts it at /var/lib/waydroid, Waydroid's work directory. Android's
        system images and its /data live there: the stateful partition is
        small and mounted noexec. Existing partitions are not changed; only a
        GPT entry is added and the backup GPT moves to the end of the card.
        If the card was reflashed with the same layout, the partition's file
        system is found again and kept. The eMMC is never touched.
        Then downloads Android (LineageOS) with `waydroid init`.
start   Starts Waydroid's container service (root), a private D-Bus session
        bus and `waydroid session start` as chronos, drawing on Chrome's
        Wayland server (/run/chrome/wayland-0); shows Android's window when it
        is ready.

Android's session data is kept on the ANDROID-DATA partition, not in the
user's home, so it is shared by everyone who logs in on this machine.

The HTTP API listens on localhost only. POST requests need the
X-Android-Launcher header (which a page from another origin cannot send
without a CORS preflight, and this server answers none) and a matching Origin,
and every request needs a localhost Host header (no DNS rebinding).
"""

